Abstract

This document defines the Intent and Decision Model of the Memory Operating System.

Intent represents the motivational and directional layer of cognition.

Decision represents the selection of future cognitive actions under constraints, uncertainty, and available information.

Together, Intent and Decision provide the mechanism through which MOS transforms passive knowledge into purposeful cognitive activity.

1. Purpose

Previous RFCs define:

RFC-0001:

The principles of cognition.

RFC-0002:

The evolution of cognition.

RFC-0003:

The structure of Cognitive Elements.

RFC-0004:

The organization and governance of cognition.

RFC-0005:

The capabilities available to cognition.

RFC-0006 defines:

why cognition occurs and how actions are selected.

2. Intent Axiom

MOS defines:

Every purposeful cognitive process SHALL originate from an Intent.

Intent provides direction to cognitive activity.

Without Intent, cognitive operations are considered exploratory, maintenance, or background processes.

3. Intent Definition

An Intent is a first-class Cognitive Element representing:

motivation
purpose
desired change
originating context
constraints
priority

Intent SHALL exist inside the Cognitive State Space.

Intent SHALL follow the same principles as all Cognitive Elements:

identity
provenance
versioning
explainability
lifecycle management
4. Intent Structure

A Cognitive Intent SHALL contain:

Intent

Identity

Purpose

Origin

Owner

Priority

Constraints

Desired Outcome

Context

Related Domains

Related Goals

Lifecycle State

Provenance
